{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedWronskian","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedWronskian","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.MvGenWronskian","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Variables","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Rename"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.det_separated_wronskian","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.det_separated_wronskian","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L148-L197","doc":"**The generalized Wronskian of a separated polynomial factors.**\n\nFor `P = ∑ₖ φₖ(x) ψₖ(y)` with the two variable groups disjoint, the mixed Wronskian is the\nproduct of a Wronskian in `x` and one in `y`.\n\n⚠ **This is the engine of Roth's lemma's induction on the number of variables**: it is what\nreduces `m` variables to `1` and `m−1`.  The bounded-order criterion supplies the two factors;\nthis identity is what lets them be used separately. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.det_of_sum_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.det_of_sum_mul","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L139-L145","doc":"A determinant of \"separated\" entries factors: the matrix is `A * Bᵀ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderivList_separated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderivList_separated","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L128-L136","doc":"**The two-sided separation.**  Operators from both groups applied to a separated product\nland on their own factors. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderivList_map_inr_separated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderivList_map_inr_separated","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L111-L126","doc":"Mirror of `pderivList_map_inl_separated` for the second group. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderiv_inr_mul_separated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderiv_inr_mul_separated","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L103-L109","doc":"Mirror of `pderiv_inl_mul_separated` for the second group. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderivList_append","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderivList_append","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L92-L101","doc":"Applying two operator lists in succession is applying their concatenation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderivList_map_inl_separated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderivList_map_inl_separated","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L74-L90","doc":"**Separation along a whole operator list.**  An iterated partial in the first group of\nvariables passes through the product, touching only the first factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderiv_inl_mul_separated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderiv_inl_mul_separated","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L61-L72","doc":"**Separation:** a derivative in the first group of variables passes through a product of\na first-group factor with a second-group factor, touching only the first.\n\nThis is what makes the Wronskian of a separated polynomial factor: the two operator families\nact on disjoint variable groups, so `Δ Δ' (φ ψ) = (Δφ)(Δ'ψ)` with no cross terms. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderiv_inr_rename_inl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderiv_inr_rename_inl","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L51-L59","doc":"Symmetrically, a derivative in the second group kills the first. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pderiv_inl_rename_inr","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pderiv_inl_rename_inr","kind":"theorem","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedWronskian.lean#L41-L49","doc":"A polynomial in the second group of variables is killed by a derivative in the first. ","signatureChunk":0}]}